Statements:
a statement is a declaritve sentence that either true or false.
there are 2 types of statements:
-atomic statements: consists only of 1 statement
-molecular statements: can be divided into multiple smaller statements.

NOTE: we can make up complicated statements by connecting 2 or more statements (either atomic or molecular) via logical connectives.

-we can say that there are 5 types of logical connectives:
and, or, if then, if and only if, not
-the first 4 are binary connectives and the last one is unary.

NOTE: whether the complicated statement is true or false is determined by the type of connective used and value of each sub statement.

NOTE: when we analyze a complicated statement we don't care about what the statements say, but we only care about whether each statement is a true or a false statement, so we can use propositional variable (sentinal variable) to represent them.
and we also have symbols to represent the connectives discusses above.

The truth table for each logical connective:
P and Q: the final statement is true if both P and Q are true.
P or Q: the final statement is true if one of P or Q or both are true.
if P then Q: the final statement is true when P is False or Q is True or both P is false and Q is true, and the final statement is false when P is true and Q is false.
P if and only if Q: is true when both are true or both are false.
NOT P: is true when P is false.

